#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129519 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129268 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "aea71b56-dd09-564e-a2bf-5606233e2819" :knowledge-point/title "Rewriting a Triple Integral Defined Over a Shifted Cylindrical Solid"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "81187673-7ac0-5e58-b248-69eeca227853" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{π}\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{2 cos ⁡ θ}\\displaystyle \\int_{1}^{2}r 2dzdrdθ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126615} {:answer/id #uuid "9e372ca8-c288-5b6f-af34-3b37cdf41d36" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{π / 2}\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{cos ⁡ θ}\\displaystyle \\int_{1}^{2}r 2dzdrdθ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126616} {:answer/id #uuid "fcb260c0-24e5-580f-9506-c5c76f158580" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{π}\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{-cos ⁡ θ}\\displaystyle \\int_{1}^{2}r 3dzdrdθ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126617} {:answer/id #uuid "05fd1905-8d14-5074-89d3-a42787783e0a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{π / 2}\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{2 cos ⁡ θ}\\displaystyle \\int_{1}^{2}r 3dzdrdθ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126618} {:answer/id #uuid "0ca051cd-833c-5347-aa63-bcbbf6080651" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{π / 2}\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{-2 sin ⁡ θ}\\displaystyle \\int_{1}^{2}r 3dzdrdθ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126619}] :answer-field/id #uuid "5de2e9ed-6a8e-5e65-b0f4-c004ab1d9bd0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126614}] :question/id #uuid "cc7985de-f7b8-5b62-b788-d731ea9f3ce6" :question/math-academy-id "q-161523" :question/problem "If the solid region $R$ is given by\n$R = {(x, y, z): (x - 1)^{2} + y^{2} \\le 1, y \\ge 0, 1 \\le z \\le 2}$,\nthen $∭_(R)x^{2} + y^{2}dV =$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186411857 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129278 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6ff4534b-6798-5f33-9823-643705229080" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Triple Integral by Converting to Cylindrical Coordinates"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "012fa540-5e42-507c-b4d7-4be584a8a37c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411681} {:answer/id #uuid "6ca03990-4e3c-545a-89cf-a853738bd60a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411736} {:answer/id #uuid "74f3c49e-b5f3-5c5e-b0db-c523644cd561" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "40" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411740} {:answer/id #uuid "d2fa903f-cfe5-50db-9712-2e6a3f05be62" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "32" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411796} {:answer/id #uuid "de2d26a9-566a-53ef-8878-c12a27c0ec9d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "26\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411802}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "74f3c49e-b5f3-5c5e-b0db-c523644cd561" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "40" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411740} :answer-field/id #uuid "f69e9c6d-9eea-5554-8872-d044f41b5762" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411849}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "47812564-3d50-5e9e-a030-d910fa667ee9" :question/math-academy-id "q-161606" :question/problem "Evaluate the triple integral \n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}4x\\,\\text{d}V\n$$\n\nover the region $R$ enclosed inside the cylinder ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4$ for $x\\ge 0,$ $y\\ge 0$ and between the surfaces $z=0$ and $z=y+3.$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,dV={∭}_{Δ}\\,f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,z)\\,r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta .\n$$\n\nIf $(r,\\theta ,z)$ are the cylindrical polar coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the formulas\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z=z.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the projection of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane:\n\n![](images/b6/b6bf808437f48af8b0853108079bde0577c92812ebf868d04a0550ecacb3376e.png)\n\nThe region $R$ is a right vertical cylinder of radius $2$ whose axis passes through the point $(0,0)$ in the $xy$-plane, and is\n\n- bounded below by $z=0,$ and\n\n- bounded above by $z=y+3=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +3.$\n\nAs a result, in cylindrical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta ,z)\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 2,\\,0\\le \\theta <\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,0\\le z\\le r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +3\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}4x\\,\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{Δ}4r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +3}4{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}4{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta [z]_{z=0}^{z=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}4{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta (r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +3)\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}4{r}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +12{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}2{r}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta +12{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{[\\frac{1}{2}{r}^{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta +4{r}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ]}_{r=0}^{r=2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\frac{1}{2}(2)^{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta +4(2)^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}8\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta +32\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =[-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta +32\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ]_{\\theta =0}^{\\theta =\\pi /2} \\\\  & =-4(-1-1)+32(1-0)) \\\\  & =40.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367701 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129278 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6ff4534b-6798-5f33-9823-643705229080" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Triple Integral by Converting to Cylindrical Coordinates"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "414072e0-1148-5168-96a6-11ffceca75b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367703} {:answer/id #uuid "5b6a336b-706c-5271-bea3-af543482e55d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367704} {:answer/id #uuid "e323b78c-c8f5-5407-aa4c-c6f8ca71ca46" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367705} {:answer/id #uuid "9e58224d-5306-5bd6-bd6f-477dd4c4803f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367706} {:answer/id #uuid "bd911543-a08f-5fe3-9053-de56b1052391" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367707}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "414072e0-1148-5168-96a6-11ffceca75b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367703} :answer-field/id #uuid "698124ee-79ce-58d1-b542-2529ea23bae8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367702}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f2e3ad27-f613-58e0-9134-a9162e7320ba" :question/math-academy-id "q-161618" :question/problem "Evaluate the triple integral \n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}V\n$$\n\nover the region $R$ enclosed inside the cylinder ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1$ and between the surfaces $z=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}$ and $z=1.$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,dV={∭}_{Δ}\\,f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,z)\\,r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta .\n$$\n\nIf $(r,\\theta ,z)$ are the cylindrical polar coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the formulas\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z=z.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the projection of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane:\n\n![](images/f4/f4c6f3acefa5d89fd5064de469952da3d68d379559bb4a6a4990a2ea24e4f861.png)\n\nThe region $R$ is a right vertical cylinder of radius $1$ whose axis passes through the point $(0,0)$ in the $xy$-plane, and is\n\n- bounded below by  $z=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}=\\sqrt{{r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }=r,$  and\n\n- bounded above by $z=1.$\n\nAs a result, in cylindrical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ={(r,\\theta ,z)\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 1,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi ,\\,r\\le z\\le 1}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{Δ}({(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}^{2}+{(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}^{2})\\,r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{r}^{1}{r}^{3}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{1}{r}^{3}[z]_{z=r}^{z=1}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{1}{r}^{3}(1-r)\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{1}{r}^{3}-{r}^{4}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{[\\frac{1}{4}{r}^{4}-\\frac{1}{5}{r}^{5}]}_{r=0}^{r=1}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\frac{1}{4}-\\frac{1}{5}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\frac{1}{20}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={[\\frac{1}{20}\\theta ]}_{\\theta =0}^{\\theta =2\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{20}(2\\pi -0) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
